<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Mito Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1609 CE – 1871 CE · capital Mito</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tokugawa branch domain whose scholars compiled the Great History of Japan and whose ideas inspired the movement to restore the emperor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mito-ibaraki">Mito</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitachi-province">Hitachi</a> to his eleventh son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yorifusa">Tokugawa Yorifusa</a> in 1609. The domain, of about 350,000 koku, became one of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a> branch houses of the Tokugawa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-mitsukuni">Tokugawa Mitsukuni</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-nihonshi">Dai Nihonshi</a>, a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, in 1657; work on it continued until 1906 and gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mito-ibaraki">Mito</a> school, stressing loyalty to the emperor. In the 1840s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-nariaki">Tokugawa Nariaki</a> pushed military reform and coastal defence and founded the Kodokan school. Mito men killed the great elder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a> in 1860.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Violent feuds between factions, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengu-party-rebellion">Tengu party</a> rising of 1864, shattered the domain. Nariaki's son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. After abolition in 1871 the domain became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibaraki-prefecture">Ibaraki Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mito-domain.jpg" alt="Mito Domain" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Barfodt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tokugawa_Lords_of_Mito_in_Hitachi_Province_(A).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1609 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mito</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tokugawa Yorifusa, Tokugawa Mitsukuni, Tokugawa Nariaki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Confucianism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mito han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1740&amp;focus=mito-domain" aria-label="Mito Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8214 866 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mito Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8534 1122L8530 1145M8534 1122L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8534 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8534 1122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mito. <a href="/atlas?year=1740&amp;focus=mito-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
